From: Runeon Contributors <dev@runeon.com>
Subject: [PATCH] ntdll: Log where a fault inside a system call happened.

When a signal arrives while a thread runs Unix code for a system call or
a Unix library call, and no setjmp handler expects it,
handle_syscall_fault() returns the exception code to the caller as the
call's status. It logs only at TRACE level, so in a default log a crash
in a Unix library, for example an illegal instruction in an audio
driver's format check, shows up at most as a failed call somewhere else.

Log one ERR line at that point with the exception code, the faulting
instruction pointer, and the Unix module (file name only) and nearest
symbol from dladdr(), with the offset from the symbol, or from the
module base when there is no symbol. The first access violation and the
first other fault of each process are logged, so that a bad caller
buffer cannot hide a crash in Unix code, and a fault repeated in a loop
logs once. Breakpoints and single steps go through trap_handler(), which
never calls this function. Faults a setjmp handler expects are not
logged.

dladdr() is not async-signal-safe; it is called at most twice per
process, and only on a path that already runs debug output and returns
an error from the call. If the fault happened while the dynamic loader
held its lock, the call can block.

Logging only: the returned status and the return path are unchanged.
i386 Unix builds keep the old behaviour; WoW64 uses the x86_64 path.

diff --git a/dlls/ntdll/unix/signal_x86_64.c b/dlls/ntdll/unix/signal_x86_64.c
--- a/dlls/ntdll/unix/signal_x86_64.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/unix/signal_x86_64.c
@@ -27,6 +27,7 @@
 #include "config.h"
 
 #include <assert.h>
+#include <dlfcn.h>
 #include <pthread.h>
 #include <signal.h>
 #include <stdlib.h>
@@ -2584,6 +2585,31 @@ static BOOL handle_syscall_fault( ucontext_t *sigcontext, EXCEPTION_RECORD *rec,
     }
     else
     {
+        /* The caller only sees the exception code as a failure status; record
+         * where the fault happened. A bad caller buffer is reported apart from
+         * other faults so that it cannot hide a crash in Unix code.
+         * ponytail: first of each per process, later ones need +seh. */
+        static LONG reported[2];
+        Dl_info info;
+
+        if (!InterlockedExchange( &reported[rec->ExceptionCode != EXCEPTION_ACCESS_VIOLATION], 1 ))
+        {
+            const char *module = "?", *symbol = "?", *slash;
+            ULONG_PTR base = 0;
+
+            if (dladdr( (void *)context->Rip, &info ))
+            {
+                if (info.dli_fname) module = (slash = strrchr( info.dli_fname, '/' )) ? slash + 1 : info.dli_fname;
+                base = (ULONG_PTR)info.dli_fbase;
+                if (info.dli_sname)
+                {
+                    symbol = info.dli_sname;
+                    base = (ULONG_PTR)info.dli_saddr;
+                }
+            }
+            ERR_(seh)( "fault in system call: code=%x ip=%lx (%s %s+%lx)\n",
+                       rec->ExceptionCode, context->Rip, module, symbol, context->Rip - base );
+        }
         TRACE_(seh)( "returning to user mode ip=%016lx ret=%08x\n", frame->rip, rec->ExceptionCode );
         RAX_sig(sigcontext) = rec->ExceptionCode;
         R13_sig(sigcontext) = (ULONG_PTR)NtCurrentTeb();
